\caption{Instance degeneracy vs.\ LP selection gain across 23 configurations ($N{=}8$; $\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$). Within Few-NERD per-entity-type ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}), degeneracy predicts LP gain when alignment is held constant.}
\label{fig:degeneracy_scatter}
\end{figure}

Across the difficulty spectrum, MV gains range from persistently negative (Few-NERD: $-2.71$ to $-1.65$\,pp, $N{=}8$--$16$) through near zero (SciERC: 3-seed mean ${\approx}0$ at $N{=}32$) to marginally positive (CoNLL: $+0.2$--$0.6$\,pp at $N{\geq}32$; Figure~\ref{fig:n_scaling}).
The optimal strategy is dataset-dependent: LP-best yields $+8$\,pp on Few-NERD where LP-F1 alignment is strong, but MV outperforms LP on CoNLL ($+0.61$ vs.\ $-2.34$\,pp at $N{=}64$); on most datasets no method exceeds $1$\,pp over greedy.

The cascade generalizes beyond NER: within NER, MV $\Delta$ tracks degeneracy (CoNLL $56.7$\%/$+0.20$; SciERC $13.4$\%/$-1.24$; Few-NERD $8.8$\%/$-2.71$\,pp), but SciERC RE ($10.1$\% degeneracy, $-5.90$\,pp) is far worse than NER at comparable degeneracy.
RE's $40$\% zero-F1 rate (\textit{diversity among failures}; vs.\ ${\leq}5.4$\% in NER) exposes a second dimension beyond degeneracy---even with LP-F1 $\rho{=}0.350$ (above SciERC NER's $0.312$), selection fails (Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}).

